= Solution
{parent=a}

Jordan form shows each block of $A^k$ is a <polynomial> in $k$ times $\lambda^k$. These tend to zero exactly when every $|\lambda|<1$, i.e. $\rho(A)<1$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=40b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The same Jordan calculation gives convergence of $\sum A^k$ exactly for $\rho(A)<1$. Finite sums satisfy $(I-A)\sum_{k=0}^mA^k=I-A^{m+1}$; taking <limits> gives $(I-A)^{-1}$. Conversely convergence forces $A^k\to0$, so part (a) applies.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

